WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:22.460
"Mad Jack" Churchill: A Life Too Unbelievable
For Fiction

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.820
When Jack Churchill died in 1996, one British
newspaper said in its obituary that it would

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.530
have been impossible to invent him because
a fictional character with his story would

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.039
not have been credible.

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:34.039
But why is that?

00:00:34.039 --> 00:00:39.399
What exactly is so unbelievable about a man
who charged into battle in World War II wielding

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:43.569
a broadsword and who shot at Nazis with a
longbow?

00:00:43.569 --> 00:00:47.839
A man who played the bagpipes while his unit
was making a landing into enemy territory?

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:53.569
A man who, time and time again, narrowly escaped
death because fortune truly favors the bold?

00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:58.440
A man who, when he finally had it with the
military life, went on to become a surfer?

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.489
Truly, there is nothing weird about that,
at least not to "Mad Jack" Churchill - a man

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:10.250
proclaimed by the Royal Explorers Club as
one of the greatest adventurers to have ever

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.750
lived.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:19.530
Early Years
"Mad Jack" was born John Malcolm Thorpe Fleming

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.219
Churchill on September 16, 1906, in Surrey,
England.

00:01:22.219 --> 00:01:26.450
His father, Alec Fleming Churchill, worked
as an engineer and, later, Director of Public

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.759
Works, which meant that he moved the family
around a lot for his job.

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:34.159
Churchill first grew up in the former British
colony of Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, and then

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.780
Hong Kong before relocating again to England.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.960
Jack had two younger brothers who would also
go on to have distinguished careers in the

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.289
military, although not nearly as colorful.

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:47.490
Churchill received his education at the Dragon
School in Oxford and King William's College

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.850
on the Isle of Man before enrolling in the
Royal Military College at Sandhurst.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.319
He finished military school in 1926 and joined
the 2nd Battalion of the Manchester Regiment

00:01:56.319 --> 00:01:59.090
on deployment to Burma, now Myanmar.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:03.740
Over there, Churchill didn't see a lot of
action, mostly patrolling the Irrawaddy River

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.470
by boat to check up on villages along the
way.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.610
He found various hobbies to pass the time.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:14.190
This is where he first started playing the
bagpipes, tutored by the Pipe Major of the

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:19.080
Queen's Own Cameron Highlanders, a regiment
first raised back in 1793.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.310
He also bought a Zenith motorcycle and took
it for road trips through Southeast Asia.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.830
He had a memorable, but dangerous encounter
while riding across India when he ran into

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:28.920
a water buffalo.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.140
For a while, it seemed like Churchill's military
career would be a short and unmemorable one.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.150
After getting bored with military life, he
retired from the Army in 1936.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.610
He went on a grand tour of Europe, accompanied
by another former member of the Manchester

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:46.830
Regiment who became a prominent soldier in
his own right by the name of Rex King-Clark.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:47.830
Image suggestion: Churchill archery
https://www.warhistoryonline.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/12/article-2255533-16b41bf7000005dc-290_306x484.jpg

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.970
Churchill took advantage of these free years
by putting one of his greatest skills to good

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.790
use – archery.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.930
He took part in competitions, even representing
Great Britain at the 1939 World Archery Championships

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.320
in Oslo, Norway.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.860
His proficiency even earned Jack a few small
movie roles where he portrayed archers in

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:09.820
films such as The Thief of Baghdad and A Yank
at Oxford.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:15.730
The War Begins
Churchill enjoyed his time away from the military,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.871
but, as soon as World War II started, he joined
up with the Manchester Regiment again and

00:03:19.871 --> 00:03:24.180
was deployed to France as part of the British
Expeditionary Force (BEF).

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.680
This is where Churchill established his "colorful"
persona and became known as "Mad Jack" or

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.180
"Fighting Jack Churchill", as he was also
sometimes called.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.450
The extent of his "peculiarities", shall we
say, is still a matter of debate.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:40.640
Churchill himself was never one to tout his
own accomplishments so most stories come from

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.960
secondary sources.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.430
When you are dealing with a grand persona
like that of "Mad Jack", it is pretty easy

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.300
to mix up myth with truth.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.690
Image suggestion: Claybeg
First off, there was the sword.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:55.870
Churchill, indeed, carried one with him because,
in his own words, "any officer who goes into

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.370
action without his sword is improperly dressed".

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.950
It is often said that Churchill favored the
Scottish claymore.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.880
That is not strictly correct, as we are not
talking about the famous, large, two-handed

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:06.900
great sword.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:12.550
Instead, he wielded a smaller, one-handed
version also called a claybeg to avoid confusion.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:16.650
The main differences between the two, apart
from the size, were that the claybeg had a

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:22.140
basket hilt instead of a cross hilt and was
single-edged, unlike its larger counterpart.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.620
Churchill probably chose this weapon because
it had been used for hundreds of years as

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.430
the full-dress sword for officers of the Highland
regiments.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.430
Image suggestion: Charge exercise.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.430
Jack with sword on the far right.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:31.430
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jack_Churchill_leading_training_charge_with_sword.jpg
"Mad Jack" did not get to use his sword in

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:32.430
combat too often.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.230
Instead, he relied on it to signal and inspire
the men, as can be evidenced by a photograph

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.930
of a charge exercise in Inveraray.

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.490
Then there was the bow &amp; arrows.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:44.570
Churchill certainly had the skill to use them
effectively and often took them on patrols

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.040
as they were a silent weapon.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.341
He is credited with making the last recorded
kill with a bow &amp; arrow in World War II when

00:04:50.341 --> 00:04:52.400
he took down a German soldier in 1940.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.580
Finally, to complete the look, "Mad Jack"
Churchill also carried a set of bagpipes.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.570
He would often play a few rousing renditions
before battle to boost the men's morale.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.100
The "March of the Cameron Men" was an old
favorite.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:07.320
Archery Practice at L'Epinette
Of course, all of Churchill's eccentricities

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.540
would not have been looked upon so fondly
by his men if he wasn't also a capable leader.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:17.740
When he joined the BEF, he was made second-in-command
of the 4th Infantry Brigade with the 2nd Battalion.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:23.180
His unit was part of the Dyle Plan or Plan
D, an effort by the French army and its allies

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:25.660
to stop the German invasion through Belgium.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.010
This did not turn out so well.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:31.280
The Allies kept losing battle after battle
and had to retreat while German troops kept

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.660
advancing westward.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.710
Eventually, this culminated in the Battle
of Dunkirk but, before that, Churchill and

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:41.970
his men had their own little moment of glory
in the tiny French village of L’Epinette

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.010
near Richebourg.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.690
By this point, Churchill was in command of
the unit because the original commander had

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.100
been injured in battle.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.830
Their goal was to patrol the Maginot Line
and secure the Allied retreat.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:57.639
On May 27, 1940, "Mad Jack" and two infantrymen
were covering the rest of his unit when they

00:05:57.639 --> 00:06:00.680
saw a small group of five German soldiers
approaching.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.960
This was the infamous moment when Churchill
took out his longbow and shot one of the enemies

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.930
before his squadmates opened fire with the
more traditional machine guns.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:12.950
The reason this is infamous, apart from the
obvious, is because different sources give

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:14.240
differing accounts.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.720
Some say the target was an officer, others
that he was a soldier.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.389
Some say that Churchill hit him square in
the heart while others claim the arrow went

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:23.250
in the neck or the stomach.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:27.350
Some sources even assert that the illustrious
moment never happened at all and that "Mad

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:31.680
Jack" himself later confessed that he had
lost his bow earlier in the campaign.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.810
Indeed, if it did happen, then hitting the
German sergeant in the heart would make the

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:37.090
most sense.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:38.930
Churchill was an elite-level archer.

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.680
He would have the skill and forethought to
go for the lethal shot on the most valuable

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:43.940
target.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.590
During the fight, Jack took a bullet in the
shoulder, but was also rewarded with the military

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.190
cross for his bravery.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:53.780
He then fought at Dunkirk where he sustained
another minor injury.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:07:01.120
Commandos at Vågsøy
Following the evacuation at Dunkirk, Churchill

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.889
returned to England, but was already eager
to get back to the fighting.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:08.960
In 1941, he joined the British Commandos and
took part in Operation Archery, also known

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.020
as the Måløy Raid.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.110
This was a British effort to launch a raid
on German forces occupying the Norwegian island

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:15.240
of Vågsøy.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.960
Churchill was dispatched with the No. 3 Commando
battalion where he, once again, served as

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:20.960
second-in-command.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.710
The raid started in the early hours of December
27.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:27.570
Unbeknownst to the Allies, the German side
also had an additional unit of light infantry

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:31.390
mountain troops called Gebirgsjäger which
was there on leave.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.650
This prolonged the battle and increased the
number of casualties, but the British were

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:35.650
still successful.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:42.190
They took about 100 prisoners, freed around
70 Norwegian resistance fighters, sunk 15,000

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:47.889
tons of shipping and obliterated strategically-significant
docks, warehouses, and fish-oil plants.

00:07:47.889 --> 00:07:51.590
Moreover, if the Nazis wanted to maintain
control of the area, they would have to redirect

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.919
troops which could have been used elsewhere.

00:07:53.919 --> 00:07:57.650
Churchill was in charge of one of the five
parties that the Commandos were divided in.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:02.699
He led his men into action with another stirring
rendition of "March of the Cameron Men".

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:06.540
Afterwards, he threw a grenade, took out his
sword and charged into battle.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.340
"Mad Jack" sustained an injury during this
fight for which he received another military

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:11.340
cross.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.150
The exact nature of the injury, however, remains
something of a mystery.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:17.949
It is possible that Churchill was hurt by
a British demolitions charge which detonated

00:08:17.949 --> 00:08:18.949
too close to him.

00:08:18.949 --> 00:08:22.650
In another version, the explosion caused the
wall that Churchill was leaning against to

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:24.360
crumble and fall on top of him.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.330
In, perhaps, the most fitting story, during
the British retreat, Jack "liberated" a bottle

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:30.450
of wine to celebrate their success.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:34.440
Again, a charge went off in close proximity
and smashed the bottle, sending a sharp shard

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.699
of glass flying towards Churchill.

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:41.110
Jack returned to England triumphantly to recuperate,
but he would soon experience tragedy.

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:45.070
Six months later, his younger brother Robert
who was a lieutenant with the Royal Navy was

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.990
killed in action off the coast of Malta.

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:49.029
Strange Strategy in Salerno
The newly-promoted Lieutenant-Colonel Churchill

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:54.930
was now in charge of the No. 2 Commando battalion
which was assigned, in 1943, to take part

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.160
in the Allied invasion of Italy.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.639
The unit first landed in Sicily and then in
Salerno.

00:08:58.639 --> 00:09:02.180
Both times, "Mad Jack" had his trademark sword
on his waist, bow &amp; arrows strapped around

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:05.500
his chest and bagpipes under arm.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.649
It was an unusual sight but, then again, so
were Churchill's tactics which, nevertheless,

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:10.649
proved effective.

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:13.759
Although Italy withdrew from the war, the
Germans still had troops in the Bay of Salerno.

00:09:13.759 --> 00:09:17.130
The Allies initiated "Operation Avalanche"
with the goal of eliminating the Nazi presence.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:21.290
Churchill's commandos had the task of disabling
the artillery fire on the western half of

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:22.290
the bay.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.199
During the final counterattack, "Mad Jack"
came up with a counterintuitive strategy.

00:09:24.199 --> 00:09:25.389
He knew that the location made a surprise
attack impossible.

00:09:25.389 --> 00:09:26.449
Instead, he went for the complete opposite.

00:09:26.449 --> 00:09:28.070
He organized his men in six parallel columns.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:30.720
They attacked in the middle of the night,
repeatedly shouting "Commando" to avoid friendly

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.720
fire.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.699
The battalion won the fight, completed its
objectives and took 136 prisoners but, somehow,

00:09:34.699 --> 00:09:36.160
Churchill's crowning moment came afterwards.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.480
Precious Prize in Pigoletti
Jack excelled as a commando.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:42.240
He liked to carry out stealthy raids and counterattacks,
leading small teams of hand-picked soldiers.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.059
One night in Italy, he went out accompanied
by just a corporal, hoping they might run

00:09:46.059 --> 00:09:47.660
into a German or two.

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:48.660
They did.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:50.220
They spotted two figures smoking cigarettes
in the darkness.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:54.459
They were Nazi soldiers, part of a group digging
trenches in the nearby village of Pigoletti.

00:09:54.459 --> 00:09:58.540
Churchill and his corporal snuck up and subdued
them, taking one each.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:11:36.959
"Mad Jack" then used his soldier as a human
shield and entered the village with his sword

00:11:36.959 --> 00:12:00.509
drawn.

00:12:00.509 --> 00:12:26.660
He

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:31.079
went from one small digging team to another,
taking them by surprise and forcing them to

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:32.079
surrender.

00:12:32.079 --> 00:12:36.379
In the end, he took 42 prisoners and made
them march back to his camp.

00:12:36.379 --> 00:12:40.470
He had them carry their own mortars, bombs,
and wounded to slow them down.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:44.220
Churchill even allowed the soldiers to keep
their weapons, but he did make sure to remove

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.569
all the rifle bolts.

00:12:45.569 --> 00:12:49.779
He was further rewarded with the Distinguished
Service Order.

00:12:49.779 --> 00:12:56.230
Disaster at Brač
Next up for Churchill was to take part in

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:59.100
the Maclean Mission in 1944 in Yugoslavia.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.910
The country had quite an adept resistance
force known as the Partisans which was led

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:08.769
by Josip Broz Tito and was causing plenty
of headaches for the Nazi war machine.

00:13:08.769 --> 00:13:12.431
The main purpose of the mission was to establish
contact with the Partisans, find out all there

00:13:12.431 --> 00:13:15.689
was to know about them and help them fight
the Germans however possible.

00:13:15.689 --> 00:13:17.990
Image suggestion: Yugoslav Partisans
"Fighting Jack" and his commandos were tasked

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:20.860
with conducting a raid on the island of Brač.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:26.800
Specifically, they wanted control of Vidova
gora which, at 2,560 ft, is the highest peak

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.009
not only on Brač, but the entire Adriatic
Islands.

00:13:30.009 --> 00:13:31.009
This mission did not turn out so well for
Churchill.

00:13:31.009 --> 00:13:33.889
Once again, he led the charge by playing the
bagpipes as his men ran into battle.

00:13:33.889 --> 00:13:37.369
However, the position was heavily fortified,
including mines and artillery.

00:13:37.369 --> 00:13:40.759
Only a few men, Churchill included, managed
to reach the objective and they were also

00:13:40.759 --> 00:13:43.049
incapacitated by shrapnel from mortar fire.

00:13:43.049 --> 00:13:45.059
The remaining survivors were taken prisoner.

00:13:45.059 --> 00:13:47.799
Prisoner of War
What happened next was a nice moment of serendipity

00:13:47.799 --> 00:13:50.149
or karma, depending on what you believe.

00:13:50.149 --> 00:13:53.939
The man in charge of the POW camp was one
Captain Hans Thornerr.

00:13:53.939 --> 00:13:55.709
He treated his prisoners well.

00:13:55.709 --> 00:13:59.199
As a show of gratitude, Churchill wrote him
a "thank you" letter and even invited him

00:13:59.199 --> 00:14:01.790
to England, after the war, to have dinner
with him and the wife.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:05.459
Thornerr kept the letter and it saved his
life later when he was captured by Yugoslav

00:14:05.459 --> 00:14:07.579
forces and tried as a war criminal.

00:14:07.579 --> 00:14:10.819
During his time as a prisoner, "Mad Jack"
was moved around a lot and this was mostly

00:14:10.819 --> 00:14:14.699
because the Germans incorrectly thought he
might be related to Winston Churchill.

00:14:14.699 --> 00:14:18.920
First, he was taken to Berlin for interrogation
and, afterwards, was sent to the Sachsenhausen

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:20.149
concentration camp in Oranienburg.

00:14:20.149 --> 00:14:24.550
He was held in Special Camp A which, as its
name would imply, was reserved for prominent

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:25.550
prisoners of war.

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:26.550
Image suggestion: Bertram James Great Escape
https://www.dailymail.co.uk/news/article-3516441/Great-Escape-hero-s-battle-payout-Nazis-Prisoner-survived-13-break-outs-fight-compensation-told-not-held-concentration-camp-proper-eventually-got-18-000.html

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.820
Churchill was not there for long because he
started working on an escape plan almost as

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:30.820
soon as he arrived.

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:35.339
In September 1944, he and a small group of
British officers dug a secret tunnel and made

00:14:35.339 --> 00:14:36.390
their way out.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:40.040
Among his co-escapees was Royal Air Force
officer Bertram James.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.540
He took part in no less than 13 breakouts
from Nazi POW camps during World War II.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:48.649
Just a few months prior to this, he was among
the men who escaped from Stalag Luft III in

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:52.910
the event immortalized in books and cinema
as "The Great Escape".

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:55.399
Back to Churchill, he sprained his ankle during
the breakout at Sachsenhausen and, while he

00:14:55.399 --> 00:14:57.689
never complained about it, it did slow him
down.

00:14:57.689 --> 00:15:01.589
He was recaptured near the town of Güstrow
and, this time, was sent to another prisoner

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:03.379
camp in Tyrol in Austria.

00:15:03.379 --> 00:15:07.350
Of course, Jack managed to escape again one
night when the floodlights were not working.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:11.459
He made his way through the Alps into Italy
where he was found and rescued by an American

00:15:11.459 --> 00:15:13.029
recon unit.

00:15:13.029 --> 00:15:20.209
On the Way to Burma
His time spent as a prisoner did nothing to

00:15:20.209 --> 00:15:22.170
dull Churchill's appetite for action.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:25.910
The Germans might have surrendered, but the
Japanese were still waging war in the Pacific

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:26.910
theater.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.329
He went off to fight them in Burma, but by
the time he had reached India, the Americans

00:15:30.329 --> 00:15:34.980
dropped the atomic bombs on Hiroshima and
Nagasaki and the Japanese forces had capitulated.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.429
Half-jokingly, "Mad Jack" said to one of his
friends that "if it hadn't been for those

00:15:38.429 --> 00:15:42.170
damned Yanks we could have kept the war going
for another ten years".

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:43.779
After the War
The Second World War might have ended, but

00:15:43.779 --> 00:15:46.689
there were plenty of other conflicts for a
man like Jack Churchill.

00:15:46.689 --> 00:15:50.970
He took advantage of the more relaxed atmosphere
to fulfill some of his less-important military

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:51.970
goals.

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.449
He took parachuting courses and joined the
5th Parachute Battalion, successfully executing

00:15:55.449 --> 00:15:57.489
his first jump on his 40th birthday.

00:15:57.489 --> 00:16:02.410
He also entertained his love of everything
Scottish by transferring to the Seaforth Highlanders

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.629
and becoming commander of a Scottish regiment.

00:16:05.629 --> 00:16:13.290
The Hadassah Medical Convoy Massacre
Churchill's last bloody moment in battle occurred

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:17.420
in 1948, during the final months of the British
Mandate of Palestine.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:21.679
He was deployed as an officer with the Highland
Light Infantry, 1st Battalion, at a time when

00:16:21.679 --> 00:16:25.139
conflicts between Jewish and Arab forces were
common in the region.

00:16:25.139 --> 00:16:29.189
On April 13, a convoy of medical staff and
supplies escorted by members of the Jewish

00:16:29.189 --> 00:16:34.240
paramilitary organization Haganah was on its
way to Hadassah Hospital on Mount Scopus in

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:37.989
Jerusalem when it was ambushed by Arab fighters.

00:16:37.989 --> 00:16:41.279
Churchill and his men were in the area and,
although British orders were to stay out of

00:16:41.279 --> 00:16:45.170
the fight, he tried to evacuate members of
the convoy in an APC.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:49.939
Possibly apocryphal, but the story goes that
"Mad Jack" walked alone towards the ambush,

00:16:49.939 --> 00:16:52.339
smiling and carrying a blackthorn stick.

00:16:52.339 --> 00:16:56.399
His reasoning was that "people are less likely
to shoot you if you smile at them".

00:16:56.399 --> 00:17:00.369
Meanwhile, his Highland Light Infantry provided
cover fire, but one of the British soldiers

00:17:00.369 --> 00:17:01.939
was killed in the shootout.

00:17:01.939 --> 00:17:05.449
Churchill's offer of help was turned down
out of belief that Haganah forces will come

00:17:05.449 --> 00:17:08.120
to the rescue with a better coordinated effort.

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:12.220
With one of his soldiers down and his assistance
refused, Jack withdrew his men.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:16.640
Haganah forces did not arrive in time and
two of the convoy trucks caught fire, killing

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.740
77 of the 79 people aboard.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:24.510
Later on, Churchill assisted with the evacuation
of Hadassah Hospital, bringing around 500

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.829
medical staff and patients to safety.

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:34.320
Life Out of the Spotlight
Churchill's military career lasted until his

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:39.690
retirement in 1959, but the last decade or
so was significantly less exciting than the

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:41.340
escapades that preceded it.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:45.201
He spent this time mostly as an instructor
at various training facilities such as the

00:17:45.201 --> 00:17:51.010
Army Apprentices School in Chepstow and the
Land/Air Warfare School in Queensland, Australia.

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:54.582
Churchill's private life was in stark contrast
to his military service as it was quiet and

00:17:54.582 --> 00:17:55.582
unassuming.

00:17:55.582 --> 00:18:00.480
In 1941, he married Rosamund Margaret Denny,
granddaughter of Sir Archibald Denny, a famed

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:01.480
naval architect.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:05.450
Together, they had two sons named Malcolm
John Leslie Churchill and Rodney Alistair

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:06.860
Gladstone Churchill.

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:10.179
During his years down under, Jack became a
fan of surfing and continued his hobby when

00:18:10.179 --> 00:18:11.179
he returned to England.

00:18:11.179 --> 00:18:15.809
He had his final brush with fame in 1955 when
he became the first person to ride the wave

00:18:15.809 --> 00:18:16.850
of the River Severn.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:23.159
Afterwards, he kept out of the limelight until
he died in 1996 in Surrey, aged 89.

00:18:23.159 --> 00:18:27.080
Churchill avoided the headlines for the last
decades of his life, but he still retained

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:32.679
traces of the rambunctious young man who treated
the Second World War as one giant, thrilling

00:18:32.679 --> 00:18:33.679
adventure.

00:18:33.679 --> 00:18:38.460
He kept busy by refurbishing steamboats, making
radio-controlled model ships and taking part

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:40.289
in motorcycle speed trials.

00:18:40.289 --> 00:18:43.860
When he worked in London, Churchill enjoyed
freaking out fellow train passengers on his

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:48.429
commute back home by calmly getting up, opening
a window and throwing his briefcase outside.

00:18:48.429 --> 00:18:50.909
But there was always method to Jack's madness.

00:18:50.909 --> 00:18:55.320
What the other riders did not know was that
he was actually hurling the briefcase in his

00:18:55.320 --> 00:19:22.679
own back garden so he would not have to carry
it from the train station.

